  "live_records": {
    "q-60753": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60753",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/2698a97b97bdcf7bc2787abce5f9b9d66c5b56b1f0b106f54eab534666fd62c2.png)\n\nWhat is the global maximum point of the function shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $x=2,$ so the global maximum point is $(2,1).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/ef7ce52349abebebe9b1cfd210a611aac821aff01ae8c05925daa1540786e887.png)",
