    "q-77754": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77754",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/5c3d176ea9646b0a614a3c6db5a70dffd045f7db0f2505be916b774ac70ea500.png)\n\nWhat is the global maximum point of the function shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x)=14.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $14.$ The function reaches this value when $x=8,$ so the global maximum point is $(8,14).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/2e7a09a2e661b61a337dda647c70ab8c542f852206f8cab06cb33d43751e3434.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
