    "q-37783": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37783",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/db99cf19eaba74238a307b280f06ef8cbf17a08ada86b293282105edd7281edb.png)\n\nWhat is the global minimum of the function shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nThe minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-1.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-1.5.$ The function reaches this value at both $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/a262ec40b439cceec6be37da8d590588012a0c3b9eed676dd09d0673540541b1.png)",
